.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
Protocol object interface

.PP
A \fBwl_interface\fP describes the API of a protocol object defined in the Wayland protocol specification\&. The protocol implementation uses a \fBwl_interface\fP within its marshalling machinery for encoding client requests\&.

.PP
The \fRname\fP of a \fBwl_interface\fP is the name of the corresponding protocol interface, and \fRversion\fP represents the version of the interface\&. The members \fRmethod_count\fP and \fRevent_count\fP represent the number of \fRmethods\fP (requests) and \fRevents\fP in the respective \fBwl_message\fP members\&.

.PP
For example, consider a protocol interface \fRfoo\fP, marked as version \fR1\fP, with two requests and one event\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
<interface name="foo" version="1">
  <request name="a"></request>
  <request name="b"></request>
  <event name="c"></event>
</interface>
.fi
.PP

.PP
Given two \fBwl_message\fP arrays \fRfoo_requests\fP and \fRfoo_events\fP, a \fBwl_interface\fP for \fRfoo\fP might be:

.PP
.PP
.nf
struct wl_interface foo_interface = {
        "foo", 1,
        2, foo_requests,
        1, foo_events
};
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
The server side of the protocol may define interface \fIimplementation types\fP that incorporate the term \fRinterface\fP in their name\&. Take care to not confuse these server-side \fRstruct\fPs with a \fBwl_interface\fP variable whose name also ends in \fRinterface\fP\&. For example, while the server may define a type \fRstruct wl_foo_interface\fP, the client may define a \fRstruct \fBwl_interface\fP wl_foo_interface\fP\&.
.RE
.PP
